the average price for gas in the us rose  to over five dollars a gallon recently  for the first time in history  unfortunately the price at the pump is  likely to keep going up market  participants love to say that the cure  for high prices is high prices  but these days the oil market isn't  providing that cure i'm going to explain  how an imbalance between supply and  demand is keeping oil prices high why  that imbalance hasn't been corrected by  the market so far  and what that could mean for oil prices  going forward  as coveted restrictions are lifted and  more people start returning to the  office and taking summer vacations  demand for oil has been picking up steam  that has helped push crude prices to  well over 100 a barrel and gas prices to  a record high the price of oil isn't  directly responsible for the price of  gas though president joe biden is making  the case that a big part of the jump in  prices is the big profits of oil  refiners he sent a letter to seven major  oil companies that said in part since  the beginning of the year refiners  margins for refining gasoline and diesel  have tripled david kelly chief global  strategist at jp morgan funds recently  calculated that oil companies profits on  each gallon of gas were the highest in  history and that's helping turn oil  companies stock prices around after  years of struggles the s p 500 energy  sector has risen by around 50 percent in  2022 while the overall index has sunk by  around 20 percent  now typically in business the best way  for a company to maximize its profits is  to produce more while prices are high  when prices rise companies increase  production that increases supply and  eventually supply outstrips demand and  prices fall but that's not happening  right now in much of the oil sector and  there's a reason after some tough years  including a historic price collapse in  2020 that sent crude prices into  negative territory  u.s oil companies have changed their  approach to business  new securities filings show that top  executives at major publicly traded oil  companies like pioneer natural resources  and occidental petroleum are now being  rewarded for keeping company costs low  and returning cash to shareholders  via stock buybacks and dividends that  new approach has meant less drilling and  more focus on keeping stock prices high  profits flowing and investors happy  according to reporting from my  colleagues ryan d zember and matt  grossman worker shortages and high input  prices have also made drilling less  attractive the change is so widespread  that a recent survey from the dallas fed  asked how high oil prices would need to  get in order for companies to go into  growth mode and almost 30 percent of oil  executives said price didn't matter  around 60 percent of the executives said  that investor pressure to maintain  capital discipline was the primary  reason companies weren't drilling more  despite skyrocketing prices the us is  now the world's top producer of oil so  american companies preferences play a  major role in supply of oil half of all  large firms in the dallas fed survey  said they expect to pump either no more  oil or just 5 more oil between the  fourth quarter of 2021 and the fourth  quarter of 2022 opec the world's largest  consortium of oil-producing countries  has also held back on flooding the  market with oil  russia's war with ukraine and the  resulting sanctions and embargoes on  russian oil have further put a cramp in  supply with supply being held down it  will likely take a fall in demand to  decrease the price of oil and  unfortunately that typically comes with  a recession  oil companies are doing what dreamville  mclute says he notes when you're chasing  after dividends you always check the  price story of my life the way it came  around the first time won't come back to  you twice  that's all for now i'll see you next  time
 

